      "explanation": "For the reactions of bromopropane with the reagents in List I, the products formed in List II are as follows:\n<br><br>\nA. <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.05ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"5.217ex\" height=\"1.645ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 2306 727\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"4B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-4B\"></use><use data-c=\"4F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-4F\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"48\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-48\" transform=\"translate(1556,0)\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> (alc) - Alcoholic KOH is a strong base and will cause elimination of HBr, forming an alkene as the product. So, A-III.\n<br><br>\nB. <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.048ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"5.09ex\" height=\"1.643ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -705 2250 726\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"4B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-4B\"></use><use data-c=\"43\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-43\" transform=\"translate(778,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"4E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-4E\" transform=\"translate(1500,0)\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> (alc) - Potassium cyanide (KCN) will replace the bromine atom in the alkyl halide with a cyanide group, forming a nitrile. So, B-I.\n<br><br>\nC. <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.546ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"7.273ex\" height=\"2.166ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 3214.6 957.4\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"41\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-41\"></use><use data-c=\"67\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-67\" transform=\"translate(750,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"4E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-4E\" transform=\"translate(1250,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"4F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-4F\" transform=\"translate(2000,0)\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(2811,-241.4) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-32\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> - Silver nitrite (AgNO2) will replace the bromine atom with a nitro group, forming a nitroalkane. So, C-IV.\n<br><br>\nD. <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.466ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"12.3ex\" height=\"2.086ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -716 5436.6 922\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"48\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-48\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(783,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"33\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-33\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(1186.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"43\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-43\"></use><use data-c=\"43\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-43\" transform=\"translate(722,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"4F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-4F\" transform=\"translate(1444,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"4F\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-4F\" transform=\"translate(2222,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"41\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-41\" transform=\"translate(3000,0)\"></use><use data-c=\"67\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-67\" transform=\"translate(3750,0)\"></use></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> - Silver acetate (H3CCOOAg) will replace the bromine atom with an acetate group, forming an ester. So, D-II.\n<br><br>\nThe correct answer is Option C: A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II."
